\title[The square-root floor of the signed circle]{The Square-Root Floor of the Signed Circle:\\
Character Weights, Exact Weil Deficiency, Two-Adic Content,\\ and the Degeneracy of Conic Witnesses}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For an odd modulus $N$ let
\[
  W(N) \;=\; \sum_{\substack{x,y \bmod N\\ x^{2}+y^{2}=1}} \jac{x}{N}
  \;=\; \sum_{x \bmod N} \jac{x-x^{3}}{N}
\]
be the Jacobi-signed count of the circle $x^{2}+y^{2}=1$ modulo $N$, where $\jac{\cdot}{N}$ denotes the Jacobi symbol. This statistic is computable from $N$ alone, without factoring $N$; it is multiplicative in the modulus; and at a prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ it equals $2a$, where $p=a^{2}+b^{2}$ with $a$ odd. It is therefore a natural candidate for a ``free witness'' to the factorisation of a semiprime, and it is bounded by the Weil floor $|W(p)| \le 2\sqrt{p}$.

We prove four structural theorems showing that the floor is unbreakable and the statistic is informationless about the split. First, for any multiplicative character $\psi$ of $\F_{p}^{\times}$ the weighted count $W_{\psi}(p)=\sum_{x^{2}+y^{2}=1}\psi(x)$ vanishes identically if $\psi(-1)=-1$, and otherwise decomposes as a sum of exactly two Jacobi sums against the quadratic character, whence $|W_{\psi}(p)| \le 2\sqrt{p}$ with a constant independent of the order of $\psi$. Second, the Weil deficiency is exact, $4p-W(p)^{2}=4b^{2}$, giving the sharp bound $W(p)^{2}\le 4p-16$ and converting near-attainment into a Diophantine condition. Third, for squarefree $N$ with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$ the $2$-adic valuation of $W(N)$ equals the number of prime factors, hence is constant on the semiprime family; and $W(pq)\equiv 4u \pmod{16}$ where $pq=u^{2}+v^{2}$ with $u$ odd, an invariant of the modulus alone. Fourth, multiplicativity holds for every integer polynomial weight, and every separable conic weight is the constant $-1$ at odd primes, hence $+1$ at every semiprime: conic witnesses are degenerate, and $\sqrt{p}$-sized fluctuation begins exactly at the elliptic degree.
\end{abstract}

